<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Servius Tullius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">trad. r. 578–535 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Servius Tullius was, according to Roman tradition, the sixth king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, reigning in the 6th century BCE. He was credited with the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, the division of citizens into wealth classes that formed the basis of the army and the centuriate assembly, and the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servian-wall">Servian Wall</a>. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> identified him with the Etruscan adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mastarna">Mastarna</a> of Vulci, shown in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-tomb">François Tomb</a> paintings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/servius-tullius.jpg" alt="Servius Tullius" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille(1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Servius_by_Rouille.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Most Roman sources name the mother of Servius Tullius as Ocrisia, a young noblewoman taken at the Roman siege of Corniculum and given to Tanaquil, wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a>. Several traditions describe his father as divine, either the household Lar or Vulcan. Livy tells how members of the royal household saw a ring of fire about the head of the sleeping boy, a sign of divine favour, after which he became a protégé of the royal family and married their daughter. Livy argues that no slave could have been granted so great an honour. The Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> offered a different account, based on Etruscan tradition, in which Servius was an Etruscan mercenary named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mastarna">Mastarna</a> who fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caelius-vibenna">Caelius Vibenna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, the sons of Ancus Marcius had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a> wounded by assassins, and Tanaquil announced from a palace window that he had named Servius regent. When the king's death became known, the senate elected Servius, the first time the people were not involved in choosing a king. He fought Veii and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>.</p><p>The so-called Servian reforms extended voting rights to commoners and defined the fiscal and military obligations of all citizens. Tradition holds that Servius held the first Roman census and formed the comitia centuriata, which displaced the comitia curiata as the central legislative body. The census grouped male citizens in classes by wealth, each divided into centuries, and the wealthiest centuries voted first. Modern scholars think the reforms were a long, piecemeal process that extended beyond his reign. He is also said to have extended the city, protected it with a rampart, moat and wall, built a temple of Diana on the Aventine and founded or reformed the Compitalia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>'s account Servius gave his two daughters to the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a>. The younger Tullia and her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Lucius Tarquinius Superbus</a> had their siblings murdered and conspired against the king. Tarquinius went to the senate house with armed men and threw Servius down the steps, and Servius was killed in the street. Livy calls this a tragic crime and makes it the just cause for the end of the monarchy.</p><p>Servius is described as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s second founder and as a kind of proto-Republican magistrate. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>'s identification with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mastarna">Mastarna</a> draws some support from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-tomb">François Tomb</a> at Vulci, whose paintings show a Macstrna among the allies of the Vibenna brothers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Servius Tullius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By Roman tradition its fifth king, Tarquinius Priscus, came from Tarquinia, the sixth, <b>Servius Tullius</b>, was identified by the emperor Claudius with the Etruscan adventurer Mastarna, and the last, Tarquinius Superbus, was expelled about 509 BCE, after which Lars Porsenna of Clusium attacked the city and may have…</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Fasti Triumphales, <b>Servius Tullius</b>, by Roman tradition the sixth king of Rome, celebrated a triumph over the Etruscans, one of three he held.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-25">25 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Fasti Triumphales, <b>Servius Tullius</b>, by tradition the sixth king of Rome, celebrated the first of three triumphs over the Etruscans.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-25">25 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition credits Numa Pompilius with Rome's religious institutions and <b>Servius Tullius</b> with the census and a new army based on wealth classes.</blockquote><a href="/state/roman-kingdom">Roman Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The François Tomb's paintings show the Vulcian heroes Caile and Avle Vipinas and Macstrna, whom the emperor Claudius identified with Rome's king <b>Servius Tullius</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/vulci">Vulci</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-551" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Servius Tullius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-551">What was here in 551 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
